@brief
    OPS-140  The error registry predates the Sentry integration and
             none of the codes say whether they should be reported.
             Sentry is now reading the flag and defaulting it to true,
             so every 4xx in here is paging somebody at night.

             Every entry needs a `"sentry"` flag after its status code.
             4xx is a client mistake: false. 5xx is ours: true.

    "ORDER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
    "PAYMENT_GATEWAY_TIMEOUT": { "statusCode": 504, "sentry": true },

Twenty-two entries, five of them 5xx.

**The budget for this one is 62 keystrokes, including `:wqa`.** Editing
the lines one at a time costs about five hundred, so the budget is not
there to make you hurry. It is there to rule out the route where you
hurry.

index.ts is open and does not change.

@keys
| `:%s/ }/, "sentry": false }/` | every line, in one pass |
| `:g/pat/s/old/new/` | run a substitution only on the lines that match |
| `:%s/\v(pat) \}/\1 … }/` | keep part of the match with a backreference |

@hints
Twenty-two lines, two different values. You are not going to find one
command that does both, so stop looking for it. Two commands is the
answer; the question is which two.
--
Most of the entries want the same flag. Give every line that one, then
correct the minority. Correcting the minority is a substitution that only
runs on lines you can describe — and you can describe them: their status
code starts with a 5.
--
`:%s/ }/, "sentry": false }/` then `:g/: 5/s/false/true/`.

@solution
`:%s/ }/, "sentry": false }/` `:g/: 5/s/false/true/` `:wqa`. 54
keystrokes.

The idea is worth more than the two commands: **do the common case
everywhere, then fix the exceptions.** Seventeen of the twenty-two want
`false` and five want `true`, so writing one rule that handles both costs
more thought and more characters than writing the majority rule and then
a correction. The correction is cheap because the exceptions are
describable — every one of them has `: 5` in it.

The same shape turns up constantly: set every field to the default, then
override the three that differ; add the import to every file, then remove
it from the one that does not need it. The instinct to write a single
perfectly-conditional command is the expensive one.

The version with a backreference is also fine:

    :%s/\v(: 4\d\d) \}/\1, "sentry": false }/
    :%s/\v(: 5\d\d) \}/\1, "sentry": true }/

It is more precise — it cannot touch a line that does not have a status
code on it — and it costs about thirty keystrokes more, which is over
today's budget. On a file this size the first version is right. On a file where you are not sure every
line has the same shape, the second is, and the extra thirty keystrokes
are the price of not having to read the diff.

What both versions have in common: neither one involves pressing `j`.
Twenty-two lines is exactly the size where hand-editing feels
manageable — it is only a couple of minutes — and that feeling is the
trap the budget is here to catch.

